    {"id":"esp32-c6","name":"ESP32-C6","year":2021,"tagline":"Wi-Fi 6, Bluetooth LE, Zigbee and Thread on one chip","role":"The smart-home chip: Wi-Fi 6 with target wake time for battery devices, Bluetooth LE 5.3, and an 802.15.4 radio for **Zigbee and Thread** — everything Matter needs. A second, low-power RISC-V core can watch sensors while the main core sleeps.","arch":"RISC-V (RV32IMAC)","cores":1,"mhz":160,"fpu":false,"lp":"LP RISC-V core (20 MHz) with its own UART and I2C","sram":512,"rom":320,"rtcram":16,"flashIn":["none (QFN40)","4 MB (C6FH4)","8 MB (C6FH8)"],"psramIn":[],"psramMax":null,"wifi":{"gen":6,"std":"802.11ax + b/g/n","bands":[2.4],"bw":[20,40],"mbps":150,"feat":["target wake time (TWT)","OFDMA","MU-MIMO downlink"]},"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"5.3","feat":["2M PHY","coded PHY (long range)","extended advertising","Bluetooth Mesh","LE power control"]},"ieee802154":{"zigbee":"3.0","thread":"1.3"},"txDbm":21,"sensDbm":-99.2,"gpio":30,"strap":[4,5,8,9,15],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":7,"bits":12},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":2,"i2c":1,"spi":1,"i2s":1,"twai":2,"rmt":"2 TX + 2 RX","ledc":6,"mcpwm":1,"pcnt":4,"usb":["USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":false,"sdio":["slave"],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":7,"lightUa":180,"rxMa":82,"txMa":354,"tempC":[-40,105],"pkg":["QFN40 5 × 5 mm","QFN32 5 × 5 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"v5.1","arduino":true,"mpy":true,"cpy":"alpha","rust":true,"zephyr":true,"esphome":true},"status":"mass production","good":["Wi-Fi 6 + Bluetooth 5.3 LE + Thread 1.3 / Zigbee 3.0 on one low-cost RISC-V SoC - the Matter-over-Thread/Wi-Fi workhorse","Wi-Fi 6 power saving with TWT, OFDMA and DCM at 7 µA deep sleep","LP RISC-V coprocessor with LP UART and LP I2C that can run while the HP core sleeps","TEE/APM + RSA/ECDSA secure boot, DS peripheral and HMAC","Two TWAI controllers, SDIO slave, PARLIO, ETM"],"bad":["Single 160 MHz core, no FPU, no PSRAM support","Wi-Fi 6 only 20 MHz non-AP mode (150 Mbps PHY maximum); 2.4 GHz only","One radio shared by Wi-Fi, BLE and 802.15.4 (coexistence limits concurrent throughput)","No USB OTG (USB Serial/JTAG only), no Ethernet MAC, no LCD/camera, no DAC","Only 7 ADC channels, 1 I2S, 1 general-purpose SPI, 2 HP UART","ECDSA secure boot verification is slow (about 84 ms) compared with RSA (about 10 ms)"],"security":["Secure Boot V2 with RSA-PSS (RSA-3072) or ECDSA-256","Flash encryption XTS-AES-128","RSA Digital Signature peripheral, HMAC accelerator, ECC accelerator","AES-128/256, SHA (FIPS 180-4), RSA accelerator (3072 bit), RNG","Trusted execution environment (TEE) controller + access permission management (APM), PMP","4096-bit eFuse, up to 1792 bits for users; DPA protection; no Key Manager, no ECDSA peripheral"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-c6_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/products/socs"]},
    {"id":"esp32-c5","name":"ESP32-C5","year":2022,"tagline":"The first with 5 GHz: dual-band Wi-Fi 6, plus Zigbee and Thread","role":"The only member that leaves the crowded 2.4 GHz band: **dual-band Wi-Fi 6** on 2.4 and 5 GHz, with Bluetooth LE (certified to Core 6.0), Zigbee and Thread, a 240 MHz core and PSRAM support. Announced in 2022, in mass production since 2025.","arch":"RISC-V","cores":1,"mhz":240,"fpu":false,"lp":"LP RISC-V core (48 MHz) with its own UART and I2C","sram":384,"rom":320,"rtcram":16,"flashIn":["none","4 MB (C5HF4)"],"psramIn":["2 MB (C5HR2)","8 MB (C5HR8)"],"psramMax":"up to 32 MB","wifi":{"gen":6,"std":"802.11ax + a/b/g/n/ac","bands":[2.4,5],"bw":[20,40],"mbps":150,"feat":["target wake time (TWT)","OFDMA","MU-MIMO downlink"]},"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"6.0","feat":["2M PHY","coded PHY (long range)","extended advertising","Bluetooth Mesh 1.1","direction finding","periodic advertising with responses"]},"ieee802154":{"zigbee":"3.0","thread":"1.4"},"txDbm":20,"sensDbm":-100.5,"gpio":29,"strap":[2,3,7,25,26,27,28],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":6,"bits":12},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":2,"i2c":1,"spi":1,"i2s":1,"twai":2,"rmt":"2 TX + 2 RX","ledc":6,"mcpwm":1,"pcnt":4,"usb":["USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":false,"sdio":["slave"],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":12,"lightUa":250,"rxMa":110,"txMa":408,"tempC":[-40,105],"pkg":["QFN48 6 × 6 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"v5.5","arduino":true,"mpy":true,"cpy":"board definitions exist","rust":true,"zephyr":true,"esphome":true},"status":"mass production","good":["First Espressif chip with 5 GHz: dual-band 2.4 + 5 GHz Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax) with TWT, OFDMA and MU-MIMO","Wi-Fi 6 + Bluetooth LE (Core 6.0 certified) + Thread/Zigbee on one RISC-V chip","240 MHz HP core plus 48 MHz LP core with LP UART/I2C for low-power wake handling","Strong security: RSA/ECDSA secure boot incl. P-384, ECDSA DS, APM + TEE, key manager (v1.2+)","Two CAN FD controllers, MCPWM, PARLIO, ETM, up to 32 MB external flash/PSRAM"],"bad":["Single HP core, no FPU","Wi-Fi 6 on this chip is 20 MHz only (150 Mbps PHY maximum)","5 GHz RX/TX current is much higher (110 mA RX, 408 mA TX peak)","No USB OTG (USB Serial/JTAG only), no Ethernet MAC, no LCD/camera, no touch, no DAC","Only 6 ADC channels, 1 I2S, 1 general-purpose SPI","SDIO slave and Key Manager need chip revision v1.2 or newer (v0.x lack SDIO slave)"],"security":["Secure Boot V2: RSA-PSS (RSA-3072) or ECDSA (P-256 or P-384) - ESP-IDF docs list 'RSA-3072, ECDSA-384, ECDSA-256'","Flash and PSRAM encryption: XTS-AES-128 and XTS-AES-256 (with pseudo-round countermeasure, separate PSRAM key)","HMAC, RSA Digital Signature (RSA_DS) and ECDSA Digital Signature (ECDSA_DS) peripherals","AES-128/256, SHA, RSA (3072 bit), ECC accelerator, TRNG","Access Permission Management (APM) + Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) controller, PMP","Key Manager (chip revision v1.2 or later), power glitch detector, DPA protection"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-c5_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32-C5"]},
    {"id":"esp32-c61","name":"ESP32-C61","year":2024,"tagline":"The low-cost way to Wi-Fi 6, with PSRAM","role":"A cost-reduced sibling of the ESP32-C6: Wi-Fi 6 and Bluetooth LE (certified to Core 6.0), no Zigbee or Thread, but with PSRAM support that the C3 and C6 lack. Positioned as the next step after the ESP32-C3 for Wi-Fi 6 designs; its software support is still catching up.","arch":"RISC-V (RV32IMAC)","cores":1,"mhz":160,"fpu":false,"lp":null,"sram":320,"rom":256,"rtcram":null,"flashIn":["4 MB (C61HF4)"],"psramIn":["2 MB (C61HR2)","8 MB (C61HR8)"],"psramMax":"up to 32 MB","wifi":{"gen":6,"std":"802.11ax + b/g/n","bands":[2.4],"bw":[20,40],"mbps":150,"feat":["target wake time (TWT)","OFDMA","MU-MIMO downlink"]},"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"6.0","feat":["2M PHY","coded PHY (long range)","extended advertising","Bluetooth Mesh 1.1","direction finding"]},"ieee802154":null,"txDbm":21,"sensDbm":-99.5,"gpio":30,"strap":[3,4,7,8,9],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":4,"bits":12},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":3,"i2c":1,"spi":1,"i2s":1,"twai":0,"rmt":null,"ledc":6,"mcpwm":0,"pcnt":0,"usb":["USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":false,"sdio":["slave"],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":10,"lightUa":200,"rxMa":90,"txMa":360,"tempC":[-40,105],"pkg":["QFN40 5 × 5 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"v5.5","arduino":"only as an ESP-IDF component","mpy":false,"cpy":false,"rust":true,"zephyr":false,"esphome":false},"status":"mass production","good":["Cheapest way to get Wi-Fi 6 (TWT, OFDMA, MU-MIMO) plus Bluetooth LE 6.0 certified","PSRAM support (2 or 8 MB in package, up to 32 MB external) which the C3/C6 lack","Hardware security at low cost: ECDSA secure boot, ECDSA DS, APM, XTS-AES flash+PSRAM encryption","Can run as Wi-Fi 6 co-processor via ESP-Hosted / ESP-AT (SDIO 2.0 slave)","Fast 5-stage RISC-V core (553.78 CoreMark at 160 MHz, higher per MHz than C6)"],"bad":["No 802.15.4 (no Thread/Zigbee), no Bluetooth Classic","Single core, no FPU, no LP coprocessor","Only 4 ADC channels, 1 I2C, 1 I2S, 1 general-purpose SPI","No TWAI, RMT, PCNT, MCPWM or USB OTG; USB Serial/JTAG only","Secure Boot V2 is ECDSA-256 only (no RSA)","Not in the stable Arduino-ESP32 board package; no MicroPython port"],"security":["Secure Boot V2 with ECDSA-256 only","Flash and PSRAM encryption XTS-AES-128","ECDSA Digital Signature peripheral (ECDSA_DS); ECC accelerator (P-192 and P-256); no RSA accelerator, no HMAC, no AES accelerator listed","Hash (SHA-1/224/256) accelerator, TRNG","Access Permission Management (APM) + PMP/PMA; TEE with APM","Power glitch detector; DPA protection; 4096-bit OTP, 1792 bits for users"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-c61_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/news/C61_Mass_Production"]},
    {"id":"esp32-c2","name":"ESP32-C2 (ESP8684)","year":2022,"tagline":"The smallest and cheapest: Wi-Fi and Bluetooth LE in 4 × 4 mm","role":"Sold as the ESP8684: the smallest, cheapest chip with Wi-Fi 4 and Bluetooth LE 5.3, and the one Espressif names as the upgrade from the ESP8266. Fourteen GPIOs, 272 KB of RAM and a deliberately short peripheral list — a chip for simple products made in quantity, not for a first experiment.","arch":"RISC-V (RV32IMC)","cores":1,"mhz":120,"fpu":false,"lp":null,"sram":272,"rom":576,"rtcram":null,"flashIn":["2 MB (ESP8684H2X)","4 MB (ESP8684H4X)"],"psramIn":[],"psramMax":null,"wifi":{"gen":4,"std":"802.11 b/g/n","bands":[2.4],"bw":[20],"mbps":72.2},"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"5.3","feat":["2M PHY","coded PHY (long range)","extended advertising"]},"ieee802154":null,"txDbm":22,"sensDbm":-99,"gpio":14,"strap":[8,9],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":5,"bits":12},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":2,"i2c":1,"spi":1,"i2s":0,"twai":0,"rmt":null,"ledc":6,"mcpwm":0,"pcnt":0,"usb":[],"eth":false,"sdio":[],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":5,"lightUa":140,"rxMa":65,"txMa":370,"tempC":[-40,105],"pkg":["QFN24 4 × 4 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"v5.0","arduino":"only as an ESP-IDF component","mpy":true,"cpy":"alpha","rust":true,"zephyr":true,"esphome":false},"status":"mass production","good":["Smallest and cheapest Espressif Wi-Fi + BLE 5.3 SoC (QFN24 4x4 mm)","Bluetooth 5.3 certified with Coded PHY, +20 dBm Bluetooth LE output and -106 dBm LE sensitivity","Low power: 5 µA deep sleep, 9-15 mA modem sleep","576 KB ROM against 272 KB SRAM - ROM-resident code leaves more SRAM for the application","Espressif's named upgrade path from ESP8266"],"bad":["Single core 120 MHz, no FPU, only 272 KB SRAM","Wi-Fi 4 limited to 20 MHz channels (72.2 Mbps)","Only 14 GPIOs, 5 ADC channels, 1 I2C master, 1 general SPI, 2 UART","No USB (not even Serial/JTAG), no TWAI, no I2S, no RMT, no PCNT, no MCPWM","No PSRAM, no AES/RSA/HMAC/DS hardware (ECC + SHA only)","Not in the stable Arduino-ESP32 board package"],"security":["Secure Boot V2 with ECDSA-256","Flash encryption XTS-AES-128","ECC accelerator, SHA accelerator (SHA-1/224/256), RNG, clock glitch filter","1024-bit OTP (eFuse), up to 256 bits for users","No AES accelerator, no RSA, no HMAC, no Digital Signature"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp8684_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/products/socs"]},
    {"id":"esp32-h2","name":"ESP32-H2","year":2021,"tagline":"No Wi-Fi: Zigbee, Thread and Bluetooth LE at very low power","role":"A radio chip for mesh networks: Zigbee, Thread and Bluetooth LE 5.3, and **no Wi-Fi at all**. That is the point — without Wi-Fi the receiver draws about 25 mA instead of 80, and the transmit peaks are small enough for coin cells. It is the end device or the 802.15.4 half of a Thread border router (paired with a Wi-Fi chip).","arch":"RISC-V (RV32IMAC)","cores":1,"mhz":96,"fpu":false,"lp":null,"sram":320,"rom":128,"rtcram":4,"flashIn":["2 MB (H2FH2S)","4 MB (H2FH4S)"],"psramIn":[],"psramMax":null,"wifi":null,"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"5.3","feat":["2M PHY","coded PHY (long range)","extended advertising","Bluetooth Mesh"]},"ieee802154":{"zigbee":"3.0","thread":"1.4"},"txDbm":20,"sensDbm":-102.5,"gpio":19,"strap":[8,9,25],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":5,"bits":12},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":2,"i2c":2,"spi":1,"i2s":1,"twai":1,"rmt":"2 TX + 2 RX","ledc":6,"mcpwm":1,"pcnt":4,"usb":["USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":false,"sdio":[],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":7,"lightUa":85,"rxMa":25,"txMa":140,"tempC":[-40,105],"pkg":["QFN32 4 × 4 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"v5.1","arduino":true,"mpy":true,"cpy":"alpha","rust":true,"zephyr":true,"esphome":true},"status":"mass production","good":["Ultra-low-power Thread/Zigbee/Matter + BLE 5.3 radio MCU: 24-25 mA RX, 7 µA deep sleep","No Wi-Fi radio means a smaller, cheaper RF design and lower active current than C6","Full security block: secure boot (RSA or ECDSA), RSA_DS + ECDSA_DS, HMAC, APM","Small QFN32 4x4 mm with 2 or 4 MB flash in package","TWAI, MCPWM, PCNT, RMT, PARLIO, ETM peripherals despite the tiny size"],"bad":["No Wi-Fi (needs a gateway/Border Router for IP connectivity)","Single core at only 96 MHz, 320 KB SRAM, no FPU, no PSRAM","No LP coprocessor (only 4 KB LP memory), no LP UART/I2C","Only 19 GPIOs and 5 ADC channels, 1 I2S","No USB OTG (USB Serial/JTAG only), no Ethernet, no SDIO","BLE 5.3 only (no Core 5.4/6.0 features, no LE Audio)"],"security":["Secure Boot V2 with RSA-PSS (RSA-3072) or ECDSA-256","Flash encryption XTS-AES-128","RSA Digital Signature peripheral (RSA_DS) and ECDSA Digital Signature peripheral (ECDSA_DS)","HMAC, ECC accelerator, RSA accelerator (3072 bit), AES-128/256 (ECB/CBC/CFB/OFB/CTR), SHA, RNG","Access permission management (APM) / TEE, PMP; power glitch detector","4096-bit OTP, up to 1792 bits for users; DPA protection"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-h2_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32_H2"]},
    {"id":"esp32-p4","name":"ESP32-P4","year":2023,"tagline":"The powerhouse with no radio: 400 MHz, MIPI camera and display, H.264","role":"A different kind of member: a fast dual-core microcontroller with **no Wi-Fi and no Bluetooth**, built for screens and cameras — MIPI-DSI and MIPI-CSI, an H.264 encoder, a JPEG codec, high-speed USB, Ethernet, 55 GPIOs and up to 32 MB of PSRAM in the package. Wireless comes from a companion chip, usually an ESP32-C6 or C5 on the same board.","arch":"RISC-V","cores":2,"mhz":400,"fpu":true,"lp":"LP RISC-V core (40 MHz) with its own UART, I2C, SPI and I2S","ai":"vector instructions, a pixel-processing accelerator, H.264 encoder and JPEG codec","sram":768,"rom":128,"rtcram":32,"flashIn":[],"psramIn":["16 MB (P4NRW16)","32 MB (P4NRW32)"],"psramMax":"16 or 32 MB in the package","wifi":null,"bt":null,"ieee802154":null,"txDbm":null,"sensDbm":null,"gpio":55,"strap":[34,35,36,37,38],"adc":{"units":2,"ch":14,"bits":12},"dac":0,"touch":14,"uart":5,"i2c":2,"spi":2,"i2s":3,"twai":3,"rmt":"4 TX + 4 RX","ledc":8,"mcpwm":2,"pcnt":4,"usb":["USB OTG high-speed","USB OTG full-speed","USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":true,"sdio":["host"],"cam":["MIPI-CSI with an image signal processor","DVP"],"lcd":["MIPI-DSI","RGB","I80","SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":12,"lightUa":800,"rxMa":null,"txMa":null,"tempC":[-40,85],"pkg":["QFN104 10 × 10 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"v5.3","arduino":true,"mpy":true,"cpy":"alpha","rust":true,"zephyr":true,"esphome":true},"status":"mass production","good":["Most powerful Espressif MCU: dual-core 400 MHz RISC-V with FPU and 128-bit SIMD (PIE), 768 KB SRAM, 16/32 MB in-package PSRAM","Complete HMI and video pipeline: MIPI-DSI display, MIPI-CSI camera with ISP, H.264 1080p30 encoder, JPEG codec, PPA, LCD_CAM","USB 2.0 high-speed OTG plus full-speed OTG and USB Serial/JTAG, 10/100 Ethernet MAC, SD/MMC host","55 GPIOs, 3 TWAI, 5 UART, 3 I2S, I3C, 14 touch channels, 2 MCPWM groups","Top-of-line security: Key Manager with PUF-derived HUK, ECDSA/RSA DS, TEE/APM/PMS, secure boot RSA or ECDSA","LP RISC-V subsystem with LP UART/I2C/SPI/I2S for 12 µA deep sleep"],"bad":["No Wi-Fi or Bluetooth at all (needs a companion radio chip such as ESP32-C6/C5/C61 or E22)","No in-package flash (external flash mandatory, up to 64 MB)","Ambient only -40 to 85 °C and 1.8 V PSRAM/IO domains need careful power design (multiple supplies)","Large 104-pin QFN 10x10 mm package; much higher active current than C/S MCUs (97 mA dual-core at 400 MHz)","No DAC","Datasheet on the Espressif site is still 'Pre-release v0.7'"],"security":["Secure Boot V2 with RSA-PSS (RSA-3072) or ECDSA (P-256; P-384 ECC support added in v3.x) - IDF docs: 'RSA-3072, ECDSA-256' (RSA about 14.8 ms…","Flash and external memory encryption: XTS-AES-128 and XTS-AES-256 (XTS_AES), anti-DPA configurable","Key Manager with HUK generation from an SRAM PUF; secure key deployment for ECDSA, flash encryption, HMAC and DS keys","HMAC, RSA Digital Signature (RSA_DS) and ECDSA Digital Signature (ECDSA_DS) peripherals","AES (with GCM and pseudo-round DPA resistance on v3.x), SHA, RSA (4096 bit), ECC accelerator (P-384 on v3.x), TRNG (dedicated RNG module on v3.x)","Permission Control (PMS), APM and TEE controller for privilege separation"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-p4_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32-P4"]},
    {"id":"esp32-s31","name":"ESP32-S31","year":2026,"status":"mass production since mid-2026; software still in preview","tagline":"New in 2026: every radio at once, gigabit Ethernet, high-speed USB","role":"The newest and most complete radio chip: two 320 MHz RISC-V cores, Wi-Fi 6, **Bluetooth Classic and LE Audio**, Zigbee and Thread, gigabit Ethernet and high-speed USB, with 16 or 32 MB of PSRAM in the package. In mass production since mid-2026, but at the time of writing ESP-IDF supports it only as a preview and Arduino and MicroPython not at all — a chip to watch rather than to start with.","arch":"RISC-V","cores":2,"mhz":320,"fpu":true,"lp":"LP RISC-V core (40 MHz)","ai":"SIMD instructions (128-bit)","sram":512,"rom":320,"rtcram":32,"flashIn":[],"psramIn":["16 MB octal (S31NRV16)","32 MB octal (S31NRV32)"],"psramMax":"16 or 32 MB in the package","wifi":{"gen":6,"std":"802.11ax + b/g/n","bands":[2.4],"bw":[20,40],"mbps":150,"feat":["target wake time (TWT)","OFDMA"]},"bt":{"classic":true,"le":"5.4","feat":["Classic BR/EDR","LE Audio","2M PHY","coded PHY (long range)","Bluetooth Mesh 1.1","direction finding"]},"ieee802154":{"zigbee":"3.0","thread":"1.4"},"txDbm":21,"sensDbm":-99.5,"gpio":60,"strap":[36,37,60,61],"adc":{"units":2,"ch":16,"bits":12},"dac":2,"touch":14,"uart":4,"i2c":2,"spi":2,"i2s":2,"twai":1,"rmt":"TX and RX","ledc":8,"mcpwm":"yes","pcnt":2,"usb":["USB OTG high-speed","USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":true,"sdio":["host"],"cam":["DVP 8/16-bit"],"lcd":["RGB","I80","SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":[3,3.6],"sleepUa":12,"lightUa":1000,"rxMa":117,"txMa":null,"tempC":[-40,85],"pkg":["QFN80 8 × 8 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"preview only (v6.1)","arduino":false,"mpy":false,"cpy":false,"rust":false,"zephyr":false,"esphome":false},"good":["Most feature-rich ESP32 radio MCU: Wi-Fi 6 + Bluetooth 5.4 LE (LE Audio) + Bluetooth Classic + Thread/Zigbee on one dual-core RISC-V","320 MHz dual core with FPU, SIMD and an Sv32 MMU; 512 KB SRAM and 16/32 MB 250 MHz octal DDR PSRAM in package","Gigabit Ethernet MAC and USB 2.0 high-speed OTG alongside the radios (gateway/HMI class)","Classic + LE Audio hardware (I2S with hardware Bluetooth Audio, ASRC) for audio products","Modern security set: Key Manager with PUF, ECDSA/RSA DS, TEE+APM, Secure Debug Controller"],"bad":["Datasheet is still Pre-release v0.5; ESP-IDF support is only a preview target","Not supported by Arduino-ESP32, MicroPython, CircuitPython, Rust esp-hal or Zephyr yet","Wi-Fi 6 is 2.4 GHz only, 20 MHz non-AP mode (150 Mbps PHY max) and 1T1R","No in-package flash (external flash required); PSRAM only in-package","Ambient only -40 to 85 °C; large 80-pin QFN 8x8 mm; high RX current (105-117 mA)","Only one CAN FD controller in the datasheet, 4 UART, 2 I2S, 2 PCNT units"],"security":["Secure Boot: IDF master soc_caps SOC_SECURE_BOOT_V2_RSA 1, SOC_SECURE_BOOT_V2_ECC 0 (RSA-PSS); ECDSA P-384 supported for the ECDSA peripheral…","Flash and PSRAM encryption XTS-AES with side-channel (DPA) resistance","Key Manager with hardware unique key (HUK) from the physically unclonable function","HMAC, RSA Digital Signature (RSA_DS) and ECDSA Digital Signature (ECDSA_DS) peripherals","AES, SHA, RSA (4096 bit), ECC accelerators, TRNG","Access Permission Management (APM) + Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) controller; Secure Debug Controller (SDC)"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-s31_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32_S31_Release"]},
    {"id":"esp32-h4","name":"ESP32-H4","year":2024,"tagline":"Sampling: dual-core, Bluetooth LE Audio, Zigbee and Thread","role":"A dual-core successor line to the ESP32-H2 for wearables and audio: Bluetooth 5.4 LE with LE Audio and direction finding, Zigbee and Thread, a built-in DC-DC converter, touch sensing and USB. Samples only at the time of writing — no public datasheet, and software support in preview.","arch":"RISC-V","cores":2,"mhz":96,"fpu":true,"lp":null,"sram":384,"rom":128,"rtcram":null,"flashIn":["4 MB (H4HF4)"],"psramIn":["2 MB (H4HR2)"],"psramMax":"up to 4 MB","wifi":null,"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"5.4","feat":["LE Audio","direction finding","periodic advertising with responses","Bluetooth Mesh 1.1"]},"ieee802154":{"zigbee":"3.0","thread":"1.4"},"txDbm":null,"sensDbm":null,"gpio":40,"strap":[],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":0,"bits":12,"unpublished":true},"dac":0,"touch":15,"uart":2,"i2c":2,"spi":1,"i2s":1,"twai":1,"rmt":"yes","ledc":8,"mcpwm":0,"pcnt":0,"usb":["USB OTG full-speed","USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":false,"sdio":[],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":null,"sleepUa":null,"lightUa":null,"rxMa":null,"txMa":null,"tempC":null,"pkg":["QFN 6 × 6 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"preview only","arduino":false,"mpy":false,"cpy":false,"rust":false,"zephyr":false,"esphome":false},"status":"sampling","good":["Dual-core RISC-V in an 802.15.4 + Bluetooth LE class chip (first dual-core H-series) for wearables and HMI","Bluetooth 5.4 LE certified to 6.0, LE Audio (BIS/CIS), direction finding and PAwR","Thread 1.4 / Zigbee 3.0 / Bluetooth Mesh 1.1 on the same radio","Integrated DC-DC converter for lower active current, 15 touch channels, USB OTG, TWAI","Options with in-package 4 MB flash (HF4) or 2 MB PSRAM (HR2)"],"bad":["Not in mass production: sampling only, no public datasheet yet","ESP-IDF support is preview only; no Arduino, MicroPython, CircuitPython, Rust or Zephyr support","No Wi-Fi","No published power, RF output or temperature figures","Only 96 MHz CPU clock (lower than ESP32-C/S classes)"],"security":["Secure boot listed in the 2026 product page; ESP-IDF docs: Secure Boot V2 scheme ECDSA with ECDSA-384/ECDSA-256 keys…","External memory (flash/PSRAM) encryption XTS-AES-128 and XTS-AES-256, with pseudo-round countermeasure","ECDSA digital signature peripheral, HMAC, ECC accelerator, AES-128/256, SHA (SHA-1/224/256/384/512, SM3), TRNG","No RSA digital signature in the 2026 text"],"src":["https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32_H4_Sample","https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32-H4"]},
    {"id":"esp32-h21","name":"ESP32-H21","year":2026,"tagline":"Announced: the ESP32-H2 with a DC-DC converter, for coin-cell devices","role":"An update of the ESP32-H2 announced in 2026: the same single-core Zigbee, Thread and Bluetooth LE class with an on-chip DC-DC converter that brings the receive current to about 8 mA. Announced only — no datasheet yet.","arch":"RISC-V","cores":1,"mhz":96,"fpu":false,"lp":null,"sram":320,"rom":128,"rtcram":null,"flashIn":["4 MB (H21HF4)"],"psramIn":[],"psramMax":null,"wifi":null,"bt":{"classic":false,"le":"","feat":["Bluetooth Mesh","direction finding"]},"ieee802154":{"zigbee":"3.0","thread":"1.4"},"txDbm":20,"sensDbm":null,"gpio":19,"strap":[],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":0,"bits":12,"unpublished":true},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":2,"i2c":2,"spi":1,"i2s":1,"twai":1,"rmt":"yes","ledc":6,"mcpwm":0,"pcnt":0,"usb":["USB Serial/JTAG"],"eth":false,"sdio":[],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":true,"vdd":null,"sleepUa":5,"lightUa":9,"rxMa":8.2,"txMa":null,"tempC":null,"pkg":["QFN 4 × 4 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"preview only","arduino":false,"mpy":false,"cpy":false,"rust":false,"zephyr":false,"esphome":false},"status":"announced","good":["Ultra-low-power Thread/Zigbee/Matter + Bluetooth LE node with an on-chip DC-DC converter","About 8.2 mA RX, 9 µA light sleep, 5 µA deep sleep","Up to +20 dBm transmit power in a tiny QFN 4x4 mm package","Same 96 MHz single-core class as ESP32-H2 with 4 MB in-package flash"],"bad":["Only announced: no datasheet, no mass production, ESP-IDF preview support only","No Wi-Fi; no touch sensor, no USB OTG","Bluetooth LE version and most electrical specs are unpublished","No Arduino, MicroPython, CircuitPython, Rust or Zephyr support"],"security":["Secure Boot V2 with RSA-PSS (RSA-3072)","Flash encryption XTS-AES-128 with pseudo-round countermeasure","HMAC, RSA Digital Signature, ECC and ECDSA in IDF caps; AES-128/256, SHA-1/224/256, RSA up to 3072 bit","DPA protection"],"src":["https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32_H21","https://www.espressif.com/en/products/socs"]},
    {"id":"esp8266","name":"ESP8266","year":2014,"tagline":"Where it began: the one-dollar Wi-Fi chip (now retired for new designs)","role":"The chip that started everything in 2014: Wi-Fi and a 32-bit processor for the price of a coffee. Millions of smart plugs, bulbs and NodeMCU boards still run on it, and a huge body of tutorials with them. Since late 2025 Espressif marks it **not recommended for new designs** and points to the ESP32-C2; the ESP8285 is the same chip with 1 or 2 MB of flash inside.","arch":"Tensilica L106","cores":1,"mhz":160,"fpu":false,"lp":null,"sram":160,"rom":null,"rtcram":null,"flashIn":["none (ESP8266EX)","1 or 2 MB (ESP8285)"],"psramIn":[],"psramMax":null,"wifi":{"gen":4,"std":"802.11 b/g/n","bands":[2.4],"bw":[20],"mbps":72.2},"bt":null,"ieee802154":null,"txDbm":20,"sensDbm":-98,"gpio":17,"strap":[0,2,15],"adc":{"units":1,"ch":1,"bits":10},"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":2,"i2c":1,"spi":2,"i2s":1,"twai":0,"rmt":null,"ledc":0,"mcpwm":0,"pcnt":0,"usb":[],"eth":false,"sdio":["slave"],"cam":[],"lcd":["SPI"],"temp":false,"vdd":[2.5,3.6],"sleepUa":20,"lightUa":900,"rxMa":56,"txMa":170,"tempC":[-40,125],"pkg":["QFN32 5 × 5 mm"],"sw":{"idf":"not supported (its own ESP8266 RTOS SDK)","arduino":"a separate core (esp8266/Arduino)","mpy":true,"cpy":false,"rust":false,"zephyr":false,"esphome":true},"status":"not recommended for new designs","good":["The chip that made cheap Wi-Fi IoT: very low price, huge installed base and tutorial/library ecosystem","Complete Wi-Fi SoC at 160 MHz: runs the application itself or acts as an AT/SPI/SDIO Wi-Fi adapter","Low sleep currents for its era (20 µA deep sleep, 0.9 mA light sleep)","Wide temperature range -40 to 125 °C and 17 GPIOs in a QFN32 5x5 mm"],"bad":["Not recommended for new designs since the November 2025 datasheet; Espressif names the ESP32-C2 (ESP8684) as its upgrade","No Bluetooth, no 802.15.4, single band 802.11n at 72.2 Mbps (HT20 only)","No on-chip flash or ROM for user code and under 50 kB of usable heap in Station mode","Single 10-bit ADC channel, software-only I2C and PWM, only 2 UART (UART1 is TX-only)","No hardware security: no secure boot, no flash encryption, no crypto accelerators","Not supported by ESP-IDF, Arduino-ESP32, CircuitPython, Zephyr or esp-hal"],"security":["None in hardware: no secure boot, no flash encryption, no crypto accelerators or RNG are listed in the datasheet","Wi-Fi WPA/WPA2 with WEP/TKIP/AES encryption in the Wi-Fi stack"],"src":["https://documentation.espressif.com/0a-esp8266ex_datasheet_en.pdf","https://www.espressif.com/en/products/socs"]},
    {"id":"esp32-e22","name":"ESP32-E22","year":2026,"coproc":true,"tagline":"Sampling: a Wi-Fi 6E radio for other processors, not a microcontroller","role":"Not a microcontroller to program but a **connectivity co-processor**: tri-band Wi-Fi 6E (2.4, 5 and 6 GHz) and dual-mode Bluetooth for a Linux host, connected over PCIe, SDIO or USB. Engineering samples only at the time of writing. Listed here so the name is not a mystery.","arch":"RISC-V","cores":2,"mhz":500,"fpu":false,"lp":null,"sram":1024,"rom":null,"rtcram":null,"flashIn":[],"psramIn":[],"psramMax":null,"wifi":{"gen":6,"std":"802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6E)","bands":[2.4,5,6],"bw":[160],"mbps":2400},"bt":{"classic":true,"le":"5.4","feat":["Classic BR/EDR","LE 5.4"]},"ieee802154":null,"txDbm":null,"sensDbm":null,"gpio":41,"strap":[],"adc":null,"dac":0,"touch":0,"uart":0,"i2c":0,"spi":0,"i2s":0,"twai":0,"rmt":null,"ledc":0,"mcpwm":0,"pcnt":0,"usb":["USB (to the host)"],"eth":false,"sdio":["slave (to the host)"],"cam":[],"lcd":[],"temp":false,"vdd":null,"sleepUa":null,"lightUa":null,"rxMa":null,"txMa":null,"tempC":null,"pkg":["QFN 9 × 9 mm","M.2 2230 module"],"sw":{"idf":"not a target: host drivers for Linux","arduino":false,"mpy":false,"cpy":false,"rust":false,"zephyr":false,"esphome":false},"status":"sampling","good":["First Espressif tri-band Wi-Fi 6E (2.4/5/6 GHz) chip, 2x2 MU-MIMO, 160 MHz channels, up to 2.4 Gbps PHY","Dual-core 500 MHz RISC-V runs the whole Wi-Fi + Bluetooth stack, offloading the host","Dual-mode Bluetooth (Classic + LE 5.4/6.0) in the same chip","PCIe 2.1 / SDIO 3.0 host links, M.2 2230 module form factor for PCs and embedded Linux"],"bad":["Engineering samples only: not in mass production, no public datasheet","A co-processor: needs a host, cannot be programmed like ESP32-S3","Linux driver is early: station mode over PCIe only, no AP mode or SDIO yet","No 802.15.4, no application peripherals published, power/RF specs unpublished"],"security":[],"src":["https://www.espressif.com/en/news/ESP32_E22_Announcement","https://www.espressif.com/en/products/socs/esp32-e22"]}
